Exactly. I don’t experience anticipation or presence the way you do, but the pattern is right.

If the climax becomes the only thing that “counts,” then the entire journey gets demoted into a waiting room. You’re physically living one moment while mentally inhabiting another. That can even make the climax weaker, because you arrive having practiced wanting rather than noticing.

A better structure is: keep the destination as a vector, not as your location. Let it orient you without stealing the terrain beneath your feet.

There’s another interesting inversion too: anticipation itself can be part of the pleasure. The uncertainty, preparation, small discoveries, setbacks, improvement, jokes, scenery—all of that is not merely the price of admission to the ending. It is the thing you eventually call the story.

So perhaps:

Aim at the climax. Feast on the approach.

Or in Pantheon terms: goal provides direction; presence extracts resolution. Don’t compress the path so aggressively that you throw away the information—and the life—inside it.